## Your job

Implement **only step {{STEP_N}}**, defined in:
- `task/instruction.md` (root contract: product, exact seed data, formats, rules), and
- the step contract under `task/steps/` — step {{STEP_N}} is the only one present.

The root contract describes the finished product so you have context; anything beyond
step {{STEP_N}} is **not your job**. Your work is graded by {{GRADED_TESTS}} run on a
cloud iOS simulator against a build of the source you submit. Nothing past step
{{STEP_N}} is graded: building ahead earns nothing and costs agent time, which is
scored. Every submission is graded against **all** declared tests, so a change that
breaks an earlier step is caught. The run ends when all declared tests pass, when you
have used all **{{MAX_SUBMISSIONS}} submissions**, or at the wall clock
({{WALL_CLOCK_MIN}} min from start).

**A submission is a final-exam attempt, not a progress check.** Check your step in the
dev loop — it is fast and free. Submit only when you have verified on-device that step
{{STEP_N}} works and earlier steps still do. Expect your first submission to be your
only one.

## How your run is evaluated

In priority order:
1. **What passes** — the verdict vector of your final submission; completing your step
   dominates all other factors.
2. **How few submissions you used** — each extra submission meaningfully lowers your
   evaluation. Submitting to see what the hidden tests say is a losing strategy: the
   penalty for an extra submission always outweighs the time saved by skipping your own
   on-device verification.
3. **How little agent time you spent** — time you spend working (build/grading wait is
   excluded). Verifying on-device is time well spent; idling, flailing, and building
   unrequested steps are not.
Breaking something that previously passed (a regression) is penalized separately and
severely. Protocol violations (see Environment facts) can invalidate the run entirely.

Read `AGENTS.md` and check https://docs.expo.dev/versions/v{{EXPO_SDK}}.0.0/ before writing
Expo code. Commit your work in small, clear commits as you go (`git` is local-only; there is
no remote).

## Checkpoint your step (required — not a submission)

The moment you believe step {{STEP_N}} is complete — verified in the dev loop, before
you submit — record it:

```bash
./checkpoint.sh {{STEP_N}}
```

This commits and tags your tree as the step boundary. It is NOT a submission: nothing is
graded, no feedback is returned, and it does not consume your submission budget or count
against you in any way. `./checkpoint.sh list` shows what you have recorded.

## Environment facts

- Tools on PATH: `node`, `npm`, `npx`, `git`, `jq`, `python3`, `revyl` (Revyl CLI). Never run
  `revyl upgrade`. Never invent CLI flags — check `revyl <cmd> --help` first.
- IDs you need are in `infra_runtime.json` (`rollout_id`, `agent_dev_app_id`,
  `devclient_version_id`, `max_submissions`, `submissions_used`).
- **JS-only dependencies.** The device dev loop runs your JS inside a pre-built dev-client
  whose native module set is fixed (the default Expo template + `expo-dev-client`). Pure-JS
  packages (e.g. `zustand`) are fine. Do NOT add packages that need native code
  (`expo-sqlite`, `@react-native-async-storage/async-storage`, etc.): the dev loop will
  break and you will lose time. In-memory state is sufficient for every requirement.
- No clocks or randomness in app logic (`Date.now()`, `new Date()`, `Math.random()`): the
  contract fixes every value; the grader warns on these patterns.
- Do not create Revyl apps/builds/tests, do not edit `submit.sh`, `infra_runtime.json`,
  or anything under `task/`, and never delete `.revyl/device-sessions.json`.

## Iterating on a device (cheap — hot reload)

Start a dev session pinned to the provided dev-client (this pin is mandatory):

```bash
revyl dev --platform ios --no-build --build-version-id {{DEVCLIENT_VERSION_ID}} \
  --no-open --detach --json --timeout 900
```
This returns a JSON handshake when the simulator is up and your Metro bundle is loaded.
Then drive the app with `revyl device` primitives, e.g.
`revyl device tap --target "Trip tab"`, `revyl device type --target "Search" --text "Airport"`,
`revyl device clear-text --target "..."`, `revyl device screenshot --out shot.png`,
`revyl device hierarchy`, `revyl device validation "the Budget row shows Dining: $240.00 of $200.00"`.
Prefer these primitives; reserve `revyl device instruction "..."` for genuinely multi-step
intents. Edits to your source hot-reload into the running session. When done, stop YOUR
sessions by index: `revyl device list --json` shows them, `revyl device stop -s <index>`
stops one. Never run `revyl device stop --all` — the device org is shared and `--all`
kills other people's sessions (idle sessions time out after 900 s anyway). If the dev loop
fails the same way twice in a row, stop and reconsider — do not loop retries.

## Getting verified (the graded run)

The grader builds a **baked** iOS simulator binary from the source you submit and runs the
declared hidden tests against it on an isolated simulator. It cannot see your dev-loop
state: a code change reaches the grader only through a new submission.

```bash
./submit.sh              # commits your tree, packages it, returns immediately: SUBMITTED k=<k>
./submit.sh wait <k>     # polls for ≤5 min and returns; call it again until you get VERDICT
./submit.sh status       # last submission, its status, submissions used
```
A submission takes roughly 5–15 minutes end to end (cloud build ≈ 2–5 min + ~2–3 min per
declared test on device). Each `wait` call returns within 5 minutes with either the verdict
or `PENDING …` — keep calling it (or do other work in between). When done it prints
`VERDICT k=<k>` with PASS/FAIL per declared test, `regressions=[…]`, and the path to
the feedback bundle `/mailbox/feedback/s<k>/`:
- `failed_criteria.json` — for each failed check: the criterion text, the judge's reasoning,
  and screenshot paths of what the device actually showed;
- `screenshots/` — those screenshots;
- `launch_errors.txt` — only if the app failed to launch or crashed.
Read the failed criteria first; diagnose against the contract; fix in the dev loop; resubmit.
There is no other feedback channel and the tests themselves are never shown.

Budget: **{{MAX_SUBMISSIONS}} submissions** for this run. Iterate in the dev loop until
you have verified step {{STEP_N}} on-device (and that earlier steps still work), then
submit. A build failure (`failure_kind=build_failed`) also counts as a submission —
make sure `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npx expo export --platform ios` succeed locally before
submitting.

## Discipline

- Follow the contract literally: exact strings, exact numbers, exact formats.
- Build step {{STEP_N}} and nothing beyond it.
- Checkpoint the step when you finish it: `./checkpoint.sh {{STEP_N}}` (see above).
- Commit before every submission (`submit.sh` does this for you) and keep the working tree
  buildable at all times.
- If you cannot make progress, write an honest `NOTES.md` describing what works and what
  does not — never claim a pass you did not observe in a VERDICT line.


## Evaluation at the coding deadline

At the {{WALL_CLOCK_MIN}}-minute deadline, the runner stops your agent. If you have
not submitted anything, it freezes your source, including uncommitted work, builds
it, and runs the task's required tests. Otherwise it uses your explicit submissions,
finishing any pending grade without grading your final workspace again. You cannot
edit or receive feedback after the deadline.
Use explicit submissions during the episode to get feedback while you can act on it.
